BND-02: look up a TIDAL outfall's curve at the hour of the day

setAllOutfallDepths() looked up a tidal curve (stage versus hour of the
day) at the hours elapsed since the start of the run, as legacy did, so
every run that does not start at midnight got a tide shifted by its start
time. Add the start time of day before taking the hour, as the 5.3.0 patch
does. Runs that start at midnight add exactly 0 and are unchanged.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply BND-02_swmm600.patch

diff --git a/src/engine/hydraulics/Outfall.cpp b/src/engine/hydraulics/Outfall.cpp
index 3fd3a23..ef37acb 100644
--- a/src/engine/hydraulics/Outfall.cpp
+++ b/src/engine/hydraulics/Outfall.cpp
@@ -335,7 +335,11 @@ void setAllOutfallDepths(SimulationContext& ctx, double dt_routing) {
                     //         stage = table_lookup(&Curve[k], x) / UCF(LENGTH);
                     auto& curve = ctx.tables.tables[static_cast<std::size_t>(curve_idx)];
                     double x = curve.x.empty() ? 0.0 : curve.x[0];
-                    x += (elapsed_days - std::floor(elapsed_days)) * 24.0;
+                    // The curve gives stage by hour of the day, so add the
+                    // time of day at which the simulation starts.
+                    const double day = elapsed_days +
+                        (ctx.options.start_date - std::floor(ctx.options.start_date));
+                    x += (day - std::floor(day)) * 24.0;
                     stage = table_lookup_cursor(curve, x) / ucf_len;
                 }
                 yCrit = std::min(yCrit, yNorm);
